aking these are polar  hormones so examples that we're actually  going to talk about our insulin and  glucagon third class is amino acid  derivatives and these are usually also  polar and it depends on obviously the  amino acid to derive from an epinephrine  it's an example of that also known as  adrenaline so now why did I mention that  why do we care whether these are  nonpolar or polar the reason why is  because knowing that information will  tell you where the receptor for that  hormone will be if something is nonpolar  then it can diffuse across a plasma  membrane so the receptors for steroids  the receptors for steroids will be  inside of the cell because the steroids  being that there are nonpolar they can  just diffuse from the outside of the  cell into the inside but peptide or  polypeptide hormones or amino acid  derivatives that are polar  they won't travel through that  hydrophobic space that in the plasma  membrane so these guys their receptors  are on the outside of the cell they're  on the cell surface so that's an  important thing to keep in mind so what  actually happens when that hormone binds  the receptor receptor so this thing  called a signaling cascade occurs which  is basically a signaling cascades  basically just a series of steps caused  by a hormone binding event so hormone  bind the receptor it causes a series of  steps to occur so those series of steps  they can do different things they can  either alter enzyme activity so there  are enzymes in our cells right so they  can make them more or less active or  inhibit or activate them that could  change what's going on in our cells  another thing that these signal Cascades  can do is they can alter gene  transcription so if we have  a that can be turned into RNA we might  do more or less gene transcription of a  particular gene if a certain hormone  causes that excuse me  so this brings us to this idea of signal  transduction and I've written out the  definition here it says the process by  